UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 2: The design process', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect. A constraint is a limit, not a wish. For Jordan’s pencil organiser the limits are real: spend nothing, fit a desk corner, and use paper or cardboard already at home. Cost, size and materials are constraints because they rule some ideas out before making starts. A slogan such as “be tidy” is not a brief. It has no user, no need and no limit a maker can follow. This lesson stays on paper: no tools yet.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nFinish Jordan’s organiser brief with the user, the need, and three constraints. The constraints must cover cost, the desk-corner size, and which materials are allowed at home.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUser Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card. Need: pencils roll off during homework and are lost. Constraint of cost: spend zero dollars. Constraint of size: the base fits a corner space of about 15 cm by 15 cm. Constraint of materials: paper and cardboard already at home. No shop list and no slogan.\n\nWATCH FOR\nWriting the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief. A slogan skips the user, the need and the limits. Another person cannot tell what to make, how big it may be, or what it may cost. Replace the slogan with user, need and constraints.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a fresh brief for a classmate whose highlighter caps fall off inside a pencil case. Include the user, the need, and constraints of zero cost, a size that fits the pencil case, and paper or cardboard from home. Do not copy Jordan’s pencil-rolling need or the 15 cm corner.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Design and Technology","learning_intention":"A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect. A constraint is a limit, not a wish. For Jordan’s pencil organiser the limits are real: spend nothing, fit a desk corner, and use paper or cardboard already at home. Cost, size and materials are constraints because they rule some ideas out before making starts. A slogan such as “be tidy” is not a brief. It has no user, no need and no limit a maker can follow. This lesson stays on paper: no tools yet.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DIT5-EVL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a fresh brief for a classmate whose highlighter caps fall off inside a pencil case. Include the user, the need, and constraints of zero cost, a size that fits the pencil case, and paper or cardboard from home. Do n Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using brief on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief.. Correction: User Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief. A slogan skips the user, the need and the limits. Another person cannot tell what to make, how big it may be, or what it may cost. Replace the slogan with user, need and constraints. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating brief on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with brief, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about brief with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for brief. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for brief. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work","User Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card","Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief"],"question":"Finish Jordan’s organiser brief with the user, the need, and three constraints. The constraints must cover cost, the desk-corner size, and which materials are allowed at home. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for brief; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect","User Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card","Careful use of constraint with clear evidence","Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named constraint, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief","Name constraint only, without linking it to brief","Apply brief to new material and name the constraint used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies brief with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on brief. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["User Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves brief","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Jordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting","Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name constraint only, with no linked example","Write a fresh brief for a classmate whose highlighter caps fall off inside a pencil case","Retype the worked example with no new example","Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief"],"question":"Which independent response best applies brief to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about brief with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating brief on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using brief on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for brief. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with brief, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief.. Correction: User Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-design-technology-y9-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Catalogue subject is Design and Technology. Proposed Year 7 alignment uses Design Innovation Technology 7–10 (2025) outcome DIT5-EVL-01, which the syllabus says may be adjusted for Stage 4 (DIT4-ADJ-01). The 2019 Design and Technology syllabus is in teach-out until 2029. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Practical work is paper or cardboard only.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise brief.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Design and Technology","success_criteria":["I can use brief with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify constraint and explain why it matters.","I can practise cost independently on a new prompt.","I can notice size needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting. User: Jordan, Year 7, homework at a small desk. Need: pencils stop rolling onto the floor. Constraints: cost is zero dollars, the base must sit in a desk corner about 15 cm by 15 cm, and the only materials are paper and cardboard already at home. A poster that says “be organised” fails this model because it has no user, no need and no limit.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFinish Jordan’s organiser brief with the user, the need, and three constraints. The constraints must cover cost, the desk-corner size, and which materials are allowed at home.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUser Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card. Need: pencils roll off during homework and are lost. Constraint of cost: spend zero dollars. Constraint of size: the base fits a corner space of about 15 cm by 15 cm. Constraint of materials: paper and cardboard already at home. No shop list and no slogan.","explain":"A design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect. A constraint is a limit, not a wish. For Jordan’s pencil organiser the limits are real: spend nothing, fit a desk corner, and use paper or cardboard already at home. Cost, size and materials are constraints because they rule some ideas out before making starts. A slogan such as “be tidy” is not a brief. It has no user, no need and no limit a maker can follow. This lesson stays on paper: no tools yet.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief. A slogan skips the user, the need and the limits. Another person cannot tell what to make, how big it may be, or what it may cost. Replace the slogan with user, need and constraints. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A brief with user, need and constraints beside a slogan that has no limits.","caption":"Constraints of cost, corner size and home card make the brief usable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Brief parts</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">User, need, three limits</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not a brief</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Tidy-desk slogan only</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Jordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting. User: Jordan, Year Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Jordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting. User: Jordan, Year Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting. User: Jordan, Year 7, homework at a small desk. Need: pencils stop rolling onto the floor. Constraints: cost is zero dollars, the base must sit in a desk corner about 15 cm by 15 cm, and the only materials are paper and cardboard already at home. A poster that says “be organised” fails this model because it has no user, no need and no limit. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Cost and corner-size limits beside the home paper-and-cardboard material limit.","caption":"Each constraint rules an idea out before making starts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cost and size</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Zero dollars; 15 cm corner</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Materials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Paper and cardboard at home</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWriting the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief. A slogan skips the user, the need and the limits. Another person cannot tell what to make, how big it may be, or what it may cost. Replace the slogan with user, need and constraints. Here is a more productive model: Jordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting. User: Jordan, Year 7, homework at a small desk. Need: pencils stop rolling onto the floor. Constraints: cost is zero dollars, the base must sit in a desk corner about 15 cm by 15 cm, and the only materials are paper and cardboard already at home. A poster that says “be organised” fails this model because it has no user, no need and no limit.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUser Jordan needs pencils to stay on the desk, within zero cost, a 15 cm corner and home card. Need: pencils roll off during homework and are lost. Constraint of cost: spend zero dollars. Constraint of size: the base fits a corner space of about 15 cm by 15 cm. Constraint of materials: paper and cardboard already at home. No shop list and no slogan.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Writing the slogan “make homework tidy” and calling that slogan a finished design brief. A slogan skips the user, the need and the limits. Another person cannot tell what to make, how big it may be, or what it may cost. Replace the slogan with user, need and constraints. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about brief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA design brief names the user, the need and the constraints the solution must respect. A constraint is a limit, not a wish. For Jordan’s pencil organiser the limits are real: spend nothing, fit a desk corner, and use paper or cardboard already at home. Cost, size and materials are constraints because they rule some ideas out before making starts. A slogan such as “be tidy” is not a brief. It has no user, no need and no limit a maker can follow. This lesson stays on paper: no tools yet. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 2: The design process","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"The design process","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nJordan’s brief records the user, the lost-pencil need, and three home constraints before any cutting. User: Jordan, Year 7, homework at a small desk. Need: pencils stop rolling onto the floor. Constraints: cost is zero dollars, the base must sit in a desk corner about 15 cm by 15 cm, and the only materials are paper and cardboard already at home. A poster that says “be organised” fails this model because it has no user, no need and no limit.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A brief with user, need and constraints beside a slogan that has no limits.","caption":"Constraints of cost, corner size and home card make the brief usable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Brief parts</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">User, need, three limits</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not a brief</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Tidy-desk slogan only</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Cost and corner-size limits beside the home paper-and-cardboard material limit.","caption":"Each constraint rules an idea out before making starts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cost and size</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Zero dollars; 15 cm corner</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Materials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Paper and cardboard at home</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-design-technology-y9-w01-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
